`Std.Ordinal` no longer has bodyless `{:axiom}` ghost functions.  `Omega`, `PlusLimit` and `TimesLimit` are now ghost functions with the same signatures and postconditions, whose bodies prove those postconditions from two new `{:axiom}` lemmas in `Std.Axioms.Ordinals`: `Infinite`, that some ordinal is not a natural number, and `Replacement`, that the values of a function on `ORDINAL` below a given ordinal have a strict upper bound, the function given as the graph `iset<Pair>`.  So every premise of `Std.Ordinal` is now a lemma, whose conclusions enter a proof only at a call, rather than a postcondition that held wherever the function was visible.  The standard-library trust list of `--modular-termination-trust:standard-library` (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/211) now has 18 entries, all lemmas: the three ghost-function entries are replaced by `Std.Axioms.Ordinals.Infinite` and `Std.Axioms.Ordinals.Replacement`.  `Std.Ordinal` now has an export set that provides these three functions without their bodies and every other declaration as before, so an importer sees exactly the postconditions it saw when they were axioms, and its proofs cost about what they did.
